### My answer to the prompt

If the caller knows whether it is looking for a doctor or a user, pass that type along with the ID and use a Strategy (or handler) for each repository. **An ID by itself does not reliably identify which table to query**: IDs can overlap between tables, and separate tables cannot enforce global uniqueness. If the service must accept only an ID, use a shared account table/inheritance model or keep a central registry that maps each globally unique ID to its account type.

For separate entities and repositories, a simple strategy setup could look like this:

```java
public enum AccountType {
    DOCTOR, USER
}

public interface Account {
    Integer getId();
}

public interface AccountLookup {
    AccountType type();
    Optional<Account> findById(Integer id);
}
```

Each handler uses its own Spring Data repository:

```java
@Component
public class DoctorAccountLookup implements AccountLookup {
    private final DoctorAccountRepository repository;

    public DoctorAccountLookup(DoctorAccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Override
    public AccountType type() {
        return AccountType.DOCTOR;
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<Account> findById(Integer id) {
        return repository.findById(id).map(account -> (Account) account);
    }
}
```

Create a similar `UserAccountLookup` using `UserAccountRepository`. Then the service can dispatch by type:

```java
@Service
public class AccountService {
    private final Map<AccountType, AccountLookup> lookups;

    public AccountService(List<AccountLookup> lookupList) {
        this.lookups = lookupList.stream().collect(
            Collectors.toMap(AccountLookup::type, Function.identity())
        );
    }

    public Account getById(AccountType type, Integer id) {
        AccountLookup lookup = lookups.get(type);
        if (lookup == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported account type: " + type);
        }
        return lookup.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new NoSuchElementException("Account not found"));
    }
}
```

