public class DoctorAccountLookup implements AccountLookupStrategy {
    private final DoctorAccountRepository repository;

    public DoctorAccountLookup(DoctorAccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    @Override
    public AccountType type() {
        return AccountType.DOCTOR;
    }

    @Override
    public Optional<Account> findById(Integer id) {
        return repository.findById(id).map(account -> (Account) account);
    }
}
```

Create a similar `UserAccountLookup` using `UserAccountRepository`. Then register strategies by type and use the requested type in the service:

```java
@Service
public class AccountService {
    private final Map<AccountType, AccountLookupStrategy> lookups;

    public AccountService(List<AccountLookupStrategy> strategies) {
        this.lookups = strategies.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.toMap(AccountLookupStrategy::type, Function.identity()));
    }

    public Account getById(AccountType type, Integer id) {
        AccountLookupStrategy lookup = lookups.get(type);
        if (lookup == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unsupported account type: " + type);
        }
        return lookup.findById(id)
            .orElseThrow(() -> new EntityNotFoundException(
                type + " account not found: " + id));
    }
}
```

The repositories can remain type-specific, for example `CrudRepository<DoctorAccount, Integer>` and `CrudRepository<UserAccount, Integer>`.

If the service truly must accept **only an ID**, clarify how it determines the entity type. If IDs are not guaranteed unique across all tables, the ID alone can be ambiguous; consider passing the type, using a shared account table, or providing a globally unique identifier.

